    ('p', 'Success for v1 is defined narrowly and honestly. A recording dragged into the inbox produces, within one coffee, a speaker-attributed transcript, a talk-ratio you can trust because it is computed arithmetically rather than guessed by a model, a set of structured insights with confidence scores, extracted MEDDIC fields on the linked deal, a scorecard, and a follow-up email draft. Everything runs offline on a mid-range machine. Cloud is opt-in per policy, never a silent dependency. If any of those steps requires you to open a terminal, the build is not done.'),
    ('h1', 'Product Definition and Gong Parity'),
    ('p', 'Gong.io is a revenue intelligence platform. Its product surface, reduced to the parts that matter to a single operator, covers five layers: capture (recording calls through calendar bots and platform integrations), transcription (speaker-attributed text with timestamps), conversation intelligence (talk ratios, topics, objections, sentiment, keywords), revenue intelligence (deal tracking, risk flags, forecast impact), and coaching (scorecards, best-practice libraries, trend analytics). Enterprise features - team leaderboards, CRM sync, forecasting committees, permission matrices - are deliberately out of scope for AMBER, not because they are hard, but because they are meaningless for one user.'),
    ('p', 'The parity table below is the contract for what AMBER v1 must match, exceed, or consciously skip. The general rule applied throughout: AMBER must exceed Gong wherever personal scale is an advantage (privacy, customizability, prompt transparency, cost), match Gong wherever the capability is algorithmic (metrics, search), and skip Gong wherever the feature exists only to serve an organization (multi-user anything). Where a capability degrades at personal scale - for example, "top performer" benchmarks, which need a team - AMBER replaces it with something a solo operator can actually use: comparison against your own best calls.'),
