    ('p', 'The architect\'s decision, as delegated at kickoff, is a single Next.js 16 monolith: one Node process serving the UI, the API routes, and the processing pipeline, with SQLite as the only database and the file system as the media vault. This is not a compromise - at personal scale it is strictly superior. A distributed design would add a deployment story, a network story, and a failure story, and would buy nothing in exchange. The monolith starts with one command, backs up with one copy, and fails in ways one person can debug. Every layer is swappable later without regret, because the boundaries that matter (provider adapters, the pipeline stages, the data model) are module boundaries, not network boundaries.'),
    ('figure', {'path': 'fig1_architecture.png', 'caption': 'Figure 1 - The AMBER processing pipeline. Solid arrows carry data left to right; the dashed risers show the Hybrid AI Router serving the three AI-consuming stages.', 'max_h': 240}),
    ('p', 'The pipeline is a six-stage flow over a call record. The Upload Inbox stage accepts a drag-andropped file (mp3, mp4, m4a, wav, or webm), hashes it with SHA-256 for identity and de-duplication, and enqueues a job. The Normalizer strips video, resamples to 16 kHz mono WAV with ffmpeg, and applies loudness normalization - a step that measurably improves Whisper-class accuracy on quiet recordings. Transcribe runs the local STT engine and emits timestamped segments; diarization is folded into this stage as a clustering post-pass, with one LLM call relabeling clusters into readable speaker names. Analyze is the insight engine: a small set of structured-output prompts producing topics, objections, sentiment, MEDDIC fields, risk flags, the scorecard, notes, and the follow-up draft. Index and Embed produces the vectors that power semantic search. The Amber Store persists everything - the database row tree plus the media vault.'),
